{{Quote|Let's see. Sorgan. Looks like there's no star port, no industrial centers, no population density. Real backwater skug hole. Which means it's perfect for us. You ready to lay low and stretch your legs for a couple of months, you little womp rat? Nobody's gonna find us here.|Djarin, to Grogu|audio=DjarinSorgan-TMS1C4.flac|<ref name="Chapter 4" />}}
'''Sorgan''' was a breathable, [[forest]]ed [[swamp]] [[planet]] located in the [[Outer Rim Territories]]. With a small [[Homeworld|native]] population, it was the only inhabitable object in its [[Star system|system]]. In [[9 ABY]], it was home to a [[Klatooinian raiders|Klatooinian tribe]], which frequently raided a small [[Krill (creature)|krill]] [[farm]]ing [[Unidentified village (Sorgan)|village]]. The raids were eventually put to a stop by the combined efforts of mercenaries [[Din Djarin]] and [[Carasynthia Dune]], along with the [[Unidentified human tribe|villagers]].

In [[18 ABY]], a cell of the [[Spice Runners of Kijimi]] [[smuggler]] gang resided in a [[Spice Runners camp|camp]] on Sorgan. After the [[New Republic]] tracked down the cell during a [[Skirmish aboard a Moraysian cruiser|mission]], the cell's leader, [[Tomasso]], moved its remaining members to other Outer Rim locations in order to protect them.

==Description==
{{Quote|We have nowhere to go.''"<br />"''Sure you do. This is a big planet. I mean, I've seen a lot smaller.|Omera and Cara Dune|<ref name="Chapter 4" />}}
The only inhabitable object in a [[star system]] situated deep within the [[Outer Rim Territories]],<ref name="Free Fall" /> Sorgan was a [[planet]] with a breathable [[atmosphere]]. It was covered in [[forest]]s,<ref name="Chapter 4" /> dense [[swamp]]s,<ref name="Free Fall" /> and [[lake]]s. The planet was home to a variety of [[Homeworld|native]] [[Species|creatures]], including [[Arges frog|frogs]], [[Krill (creature)|krill]], and [[grinjer]].<ref name="Chapter 4" />

==History==
===Confrontation at the common house===
{{Quote|Well, this has been a real treat, but unless you want to go another round, one of us is going to have to move on, and I was here first.|Carasynthia Dune to Din Djarin|<ref name="Chapter 4" />}}
[[File:Sorgan_Bar_The_Mandalorian.png|thumb|left|300px|Djarin encountered Carasynthia Dune, a former shock trooper of the New Republic, on Sorgan.]]
During the [[New Republic Era|time]] of the [[New Republic]], [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]], simply known as "the Mandalorian," fled to Sorgan to escape the [[Bounty Hunters' Guild]] after a confrontation on<ref name="Chapter 4" /> the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] world<ref name="The Star Wars Book">''[[The Star Wars Book]]''</ref> of [[Nevarro]]<ref name="Chapter 4" /> around [[9 ABY]].<ref name="Ch4 Date">{{MandoDate}}</ref> With [[Din Grogu|Grogu]] in tow, Djarin traveled to Sorgan hoping to find a safe, temporary home for the child. Shortly after arriving, Djarin had a brief confrontation with ex-[[New Republic]] [[shock trooper]] [[Carasynthia Dune]] outside of a [[Sorgan Common House|common house]] where the duo had been eating. Dune and Djarin parted ways, stating that the planet wasn't big enough for them both to stay.<ref name="Chapter 4" />

===Defending the village===
{{Quote|You got two problems here. You got the bandits, and you got the mech. We'll handle the AT-ST, but you have to protect us when they come out of the woods. And I don't have to tell you how dangerous they are.|Din Djarin to the villagers|<ref name="Chapter 4" />}}
Shortly after the confrontation, Djarin was approached by two native krill [[Unidentified human tribe|farmers]], who pleaded for his assistance in fighting off a group of [[Klatooinian]] [[Klatooinian raiders|raiders]] that had [[Death|plagued]] their [[Unidentified village (Sorgan)|village]]. After some convincing, Djarin agreed and approached Dune once again, asking for her assistance. The two mercenaries accompanied the villagers, along with Grogu, to the village. While scouting out the area surrounding the village, Dune and Djarin discovered [[All Terrain Scout Transport]] tracks. They attempted to encourage the villagers to leave, but the natives elected to stay in their ancestral home. Instead of leaving, Dune and Djarin began teaching the villagers to use [[blaster]]s to defend themselves and provide support against the raiders. After developing a plan to defeat the [[AT-ST Raider]] [[walker]], the two mercenaries drilled with the villagers in order to prepare for the next raid.<ref name="Chapter 4" />

[[File:Raider-Ambush.png|thumb|right|250px|Dune and Djarin, aided by the villagers, defend against Klatooinian raiders.]]
Once Dune and Djarin felt that the villagers were sufficiently prepared, the duo stealthily attacked the [[Klatooinian wikiup|raiders' outpost]], thinning enemy numbers and drawing the Klatooinians towards the village. As expected, the raiders pursued the mercenaries with their AT-ST, and engaged with the villagers, resulting in casualties on both sides. Dune and Djarin attempted to lure the AT-ST to the pre-dug hole they had ordered to be made, but the walker stopped just short of it. The villagers rushed out to defend the pinned mercenaries, engaging in hand-to-hand combat with the raiders. While still taking fire, Dune used Djarin's [[Amban phase-pulse blaster|Amban sniper rifle]] to draw the walker closer, taking potshots until it fell into their trap and was destroyed, causing the retreat of the raiders.<ref name="Chapter 4" />

Djarin intended to stay on Sorgan with Grogu, but the appearance of a bounty hunter in possession of a [[tracking fob]] for Grogu forced his hand. The [[Kubaz]] hunter had been able to proceed as far as aiming a shot at Grogu, but was killed by Dune before he was able to fire. After this close call, Djarin decided to leave the planet for Grogu's safety, as well as the villages'. Before leaving Sorgan, the Mandalorian exchanged farewells with Cara Dune and [[Omera]], a villager Djarin had become close with over his time at the village.<ref name="Chapter 4" />

===Recruiting a comrade===
{{Quote|I have a ship. I can bring you there and back with a handsome reward. You can live free of worry.''"<br />"''I'm already free of worry, and I'm not in the mood to play soldier anymore. Especially fighting some local warlord.''"<br />"''He's not a local warlord. He's Imperial.''"<br />"''I'm in.|Djarin and Dune|<ref name="Chapter 7">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 7: The Reckoning}}</ref>}}
[[File:Dune-Fight-TMS1C7.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Dune fights a [[Zabrak]] in the common house on Sorgan]]
Shortly after his departure from the planet, Din Djarin returned to the common house on Sorgan with Grogu to recruit the aid of Carasynthia Dune against an [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[warlord]] that had taken over Nevarro. Djarin found her competing in a fight ring for sport, with a group of rowdy spectators [[Gamble|gambling]] on the outcome. Although Dune was reluctant to come to the Mandalorian's aid, she changed her mind when he revealed that they would be fighting against an ex-Imperial [[The Client|warlord]].<ref name="Chapter 7" />

===Spice Runner hideout===
{{Quote|Here we are. Home, sweet temporary home.''"<br />"''Temporary? What makes you think that, pup?''"<br />"''I mean, Sorgan? This place is more boring than Yavin Four. I don't think anyone&mdash;even a space cartographer&mdash;can find this rock on a map.|Poe Dameron and Vigilch|<ref name="Free Fall" />}}
In [[18 ABY]],<ref name="PD:FF Date">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]'' states that [[Poe Dameron]] joined the [[Spice Runners of Kijimi]] sixteen [[Standard year|years]] before the [[Hosnian Cataclysm|Starkiller Incident]], which ''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas]]'' dates to [[34 ABY]]. ''[[Poe Dameron: Free Fall]]'' establishes that Dameron's cell of the Spice Runners operates on Sorgan after he joins, meaning they must have resided on the planet in [[18 ABY]].</ref> a cell of the [[smuggler]] gang known as the [[Spice Runners of Kijimi]] operated from a [[Spice Runners camp|camp]] on Sorgan. The cell was led by the high-ranking Spice Runner [[Tomasso]] and included [[Zorii Bliss]], the daughter of the gang's leader, [[Zeva Bliss]], as well as gang members [[Marinda Gan]], [[Gen Tri]], and [[Vigilch]].<ref name="Free Fall" /> In that [[Standard year|year]],<ref name="PD:FF Date" /> the young [[pilot]] [[Poe Dameron]] joined the Spice Runners, flying their [[XS stock light freighter]], the ''[[Ragged Claw]]'', from the [[moon]] [[Yavin 4]] to Sorgan, assuming their stop was merely temporary.<ref name="Free Fall" />

The cell operated out of Sorgan for around a [[Standard month|month]], undertaking numerous missions. After one [[Incident on Quintil|mission]] to the planet [[Quintil]], in which the cell rescued Tomasso, Dameron returned the smugglers to Sorgan aboard the ''Ragged Claw'' by [[hyperspace skipping]] in order to evade members of the rival [[Zualjinn Syndicate]]. After repairing the [[light freighter]], Dameron and Bliss briefly left Sorgan so that the latter could practice flying the vessel, but were called back to the surface by Tomasso.<ref name="Free Fall" />

During a further [[Skirmish aboard a Moraysian cruiser|mission]] aboard a [[Unidentified Moraysian cruiser|Moraysian cruiser]], [[Vigilch]] was killed, and [[New Republic officer|Officer]] [[Sela Trune]] of the [[New Republic Security Bureau]] managed to track down the Spice Runner cell, forcing Dameron and Bliss to escape and leave Gan and Tri behind on the [[cruiser]]. Dameron had Bliss and the [[EV-series]] [[droid]] [[EV-6B6]] return to Sorgan aboard a [[BTL-S3 Y-wing starfighter]] and followed in the ''Ragged Claw'' shortly afterward. Following the mission, the cell departed from Sorgan, traveling to various safe houses across the Outer Rim to avoid the New Republic.<ref name="Free Fall" />

==Inhabitants==
An underdeveloped planet with minimal governance and a mostly agrarian society,<ref name="Free Fall" /> Sorgan had a low population density and was home to simple human farmers who resided in villages.<ref name="Chapter 4" /> It was estimated to have less than 10,000 inhabitants.<ref name="Handbook" /> A common house on Sorgan was owned by<ref name="Chapter 4" /> [[Dara Vish]],<ref name="Handbook" /> who was welcoming to newcomers such as Din Djarin and Carasythia Dune<ref name="Chapter 4" /> in 9 [[Galactic Standard Calendar|ABY]].<ref name="Ch4 Date" /> The local tribe of Klatooinian raiders were more advanced than their human counterparts, making use of machinery and weapons such as an AT-ST, an anti-infantry walker used by the [[Galactic Empire]], to terrorize and loot the local villages.<ref name="Chapter 4" />

==Locations==
[[File:Sorgan_Village_The_Mandalorian.png|thumb|right|250px|A krill farming village]]
Sorgan had no large settlements, industrial centers, or [[Spaceport|star ports]]. The planet was home to a common house that served as a hub for visitors, which served [[bone broth]] and [[spotchka]],<ref name="Chapter 4" /> and hosted a fighting ring for entertainment.<ref name="Chapter 7" /> Further into the wilderness, a village of krill famers resided. For a period<ref name="Chapter 4" /> around 9 ABY,<ref name="Ch4 Date" /> the village was raided by a Klatooinian tribe, who had set up a base camp near the farms. As part of their plan to repel the raiders, Djarin and Dune snuck into the camp and caused chaos, hoping to lure out the walker.<ref name="Chapter 4" /> In 18 ABY,<ref name="PD:FF Date" /> a cell of the Spice Runners of Kijimi operated from a camp on Sorgan.<ref name="Free Fall" />

==Behind the scenes==
Sorgan first appeared in ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'' [[Chapter 4: Sanctuary]], which aired on streaming platform [[Disney+]]<ref name="Chapter 4" /> on [[November 29]], [[2019]].<ref name="DTCI">{{WebCite|url=https://lumiere-a.akamaihd.net/v1/documents/the_mandalorian_project_one-sheet_10_ec1e005f.pdf|text=The Mandalorian Media Kit|date=2019-09-24|format=[[Wikipedia:PDF|PDF]]|work=[[Wikipedia:Walt Disney Direct-to-Consumer & International|Walt Disney Direct-to-Consumer & International]]|pages=[https://lumiere-a.akamaihd.net/v1/documents/the_mandalorian_project_one-sheet_10_ec1e005f.pdf#page=1 p. 1]|archivedate=20210623202717}}</ref>

